Hi all,

I've run into this issue on 7.0.10, 7.1.6, 7.1.8 and 7.1.9 (specifically
Linux 7.1.9-arch1-2), as well as LTS kernel 6.18.45.

to Mario's questions (thank you for them; they were captured on the
7.1.9 kernel):

> Can we please get some kernel logs for these two cases on the command
line?
>
> thunderbolt.dyndbg=+p

Here you are, the system does not load the graphical interface:
https://gist.github.com/Poirot12345T/be6b7849e039687e248b8c302615d9f3

> thunderbolt.dyndbg=+p thunderbolt.host_reset=false

In this case, graphical interface loaded successfully:
https://gist.github.com/Poirot12345T/c63a1a3b2b6609b30a1182e7681fbec1

> Also what is the value for:
>
> $ cat /sys/bus/thunderbolt/devices/domain0/iommu_dma_protection

That returns as "1" on my system.

I've also done some digging myself, here's what I found out:

(note: I will use host_reset=0 in this post, even though the thread used
host_reset=false. These two values are functionally identical.)

Originally tracked down in amdgpu bug tracker
(https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/drm/amd/-/work_items/5632) and based on
the suggestion to use thunderbolt.host_reset=false mentioned in this
thread, I traced back the issue to commit 59a54c5f3dbd (authored by
Sanath S, CC'd) which touched drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c (Thunderbolt/USB
subsystem, added Mika Westerberg as a maintainer and CC'd linux-usb
mailing list), more specifically function tb_start() and this code block:

    /*
     * Boot firmware might have created tunnels of its own. Since we
     * cannot be sure they are usable for us, tear them down and
     * reset the ports to handle it as new hotplug for USB4 v1
     * routers (for USB4 v2 and beyond we already do host reset).
     */
    if (reset && tb_switch_is_usb4(tb->root_switch)) {
        discover = false;
        if (usb4_switch_version(tb->root_switch) == 1)
            tb_switch_reset(tb->root_switch);
    }

This block should be located between lines 3060 and 3070 of the above
mentioned file as of the time I am writing this post.

I was able to reproduce this issue using AMD RX 7600 (journalctl dump
link:
https://gist.github.com/Poirot12345T/909f3d071f87655d875445e536da74d3).
I have also tried NVIDIA RTX 5060 with the nvidia-open driver which did
not trigger this behavior in the default setting. (journalctl:
https://gist.github.com/Poirot12345T/4deeae10508e3971521aca9e612b5737)

I tried to do some other digging, which led me to this post on Framework
Forum:
https://community.frame.work/t/solved-nvidia-kernel-modules-loaded-before-thunderbolt-3-egpu-modules/67361.
In this forum post, the post author experienced similar issue on NVIDIA
eGPU. This post led me to think that this behavior shouldn't be AMD
specific. So I forced early loading of NVIDIA drivers in mkinitcpio.conf
(further confirming the finding is not vendor-specific, but based on the
question in which phase is the driver loaded) and was able to reproduce
the issue (journal dump:
https://gist.github.com/Poirot12345T/65d6db738ef82ba2c11e977b2ca6a52b).
Notably, I experienced that NVIDIA was able to show me the SDDM Greeter
for login. After successful login in SDDM Greeter, the sddm-helper
crashed, resulting in Window Manager not loading up at all and thus me
being unable to use the system. This tunnel reset can be cleanly seen in
these lines:

Aug 30 11:17:08 archlinux kernel: nvidia 0000:63:00.0: enabling device
(0000 -> 0003)
Aug 30 11:17:11 ARCH-FRAMEWORK kernel: ACPI: bus type thunderbolt registered
Aug 30 11:17:11 ARCH-FRAMEWORK kernel: NVRM: Xid (PCI:0000:63:00): 79,
pid=358, name=kworker/8:2, GPU has fallen off the bus.
Aug 30 11:17:11 ARCH-FRAMEWORK kernel: NVRM: GPU 0000:63:00.0: GPU has
fallen off the bus.

I tried to reproduce the very same hang on nouveau driver as well, but
that got reproduced with notably less severe outcome as visible in the
journal dump
(https://gist.github.com/Poirot12345T/f93a768d271117bf425f0aa6ec065795),
the system remained usable and I was able to successfully log in even
though the reset happened, as visible in below cited lines from the
journal log:

Aug 30 10:26:25 ARCH-FRAMEWORK kernel: pcieport 0000:00:01.2: pciehp:
Slot(0-1): Link Down
Aug 30 10:26:25 ARCH-FRAMEWORK kernel: pcieport 0000:00:01.2: pciehp:
Slot(0-1): Card not present
Aug 30 10:26:26 ARCH-FRAMEWORK kernel: WARNING:
drivers/gpu/drm/nouveau/nvif/timer.c:36 at
nvif_timer_wait_test+0x5f/0x70 [nouveau], CPU#8: kworker/8:1/139
Aug 30 10:26:26 ARCH-FRAMEWORK kernel: nouveau 0000:63:00.0: drm: core
notifier timeout

Notably, it is also worth mentioning that I needed to force nouveau to
load early, unlike amdgpu, which got loaded early automatically due to
the kms hook.

For AMD case (as it was the clearest failure mode of them all), I tried
to boot up with setting up kernel parameter thunderbolt.host_reset=0 to
force the above mentioned code block not to execute which resulted in
the eGPU connection not being torn down and successfully booting up to
the graphical interface. (journalctl:
https://gist.github.com/Poirot12345T/6757c121f93aed7f0047b7d8a741bd69).

Given the tunnel reset issue in all of amdgpu, nvidia-open and nouveau
seems to be triggered by the same behavior of the Thunderbolt driver
(though reacting differently), this suggests two options for fixing this
issue properly (not just slamming host_reset=0): either forcing the GPU
drivers to load late to skip this race condition, or detecting which
tunnel is usable and thus does not need to be reset.

I am against following the first named option, as the fix will blast
well outside of Thunderbolt scope, forcing any GPU driver to do the late
start (even for Intel graphics, which I sadly didn't have the chance to
test at all). The other option (detecting usable tunnels) seems more
rational as the fix stays within Thunderbolt code space, not forcing a
particular behavior to any GPU driver.

I am not experienced enough to tell if this approach is feasible at this
time in the tb_start() function, but it looks like the direction we
should want to take.

I also tried to build and boot kernel version 6.8.7 (supposedly the last
kernel before this regression was backported to stable 6.8.8) to
directly confirm my findings are tied to this thread. After solving some
unrelated toolchain issues (old kernel vs. modern GCC and C standard), I
got a bootable 6.8.7, but cold-booting made it crash immediately without
any easy to retrieve logs, and booting without the eGPU let me log in to
SDDM (which was already kinda laggy/freezing) and the system crashed
shortly after, likely due to unrelated issues of packages version
mismatch (otherwise up-to-date system mixed up with much older kernel).
As I wasn't able to gather a clean result from this test either way, I
wasn't able to directly confirm this via bisection. If that should
become useful, I will try it again.

I am more than happy to help with anything that makes this issue getting
solved, whether it is testing patches, providing debug info in any kind,
or anything else needed.

For the record, I came across report of (Intel-based) Mac mini and
NVIDIA eGPU not talking to each other well
(https://lore.kernel.org/all/8653B042-F73C-46F4-8D3E-79D9F0CC1250@icloud.com/).
As the mechanism described in the Mac report seems vastly different from
what I experienced, I don't think these issues are related.

All journalctl logs are also provided as an attachment to this message
as a backup.

This is my first time writing to a Linux Kernel Mailing List - please
let me know if I missed any expected format or process.

I am not subscribed to any mailing lists above, please keep me in CC on
this topic.

Kind regards,

František Tichý

student of Faculty of Information Technology, Czech Technical University
